# P084 research notes (running log; snapshot after each stage)
Started 2026-10-18. Pool: US customs duty ($214B TTM thru Jul 2026) + duty-mitigation services.
## Stage 0 plan (before searching)
Candidate angles to test (not conclusions):
- insider: drawback specialist (assignment chains, 5-yr lookback, 301 eligible? 232/IEEPA barred?), licensed broker (duty-advance A/R book, bond commissions, can't audit own filings)
- pattern: AI-native broker roll-up (buy-the-services-firm), duty financing via repayment channel (PMS ACH), legal-shock protest factory (Sec 122/301)
- dilemma: Big Four can't take contingent fees from audit clients; brokers can't audit own filings; Altana sells to CBP so importers won't hand it supplier data (channel conflict)
- shock: Sec 232 derivative metal-content declaration data task; DOJ trade-fraud/FCA + prior disclosure; bond sufficiency
Questions: Q1 market size (entries, importers, brokers, fees); Q2 drawback paid/unclaimed + eligibility; Q3 duty financing startups; Q4 AI broker startups/roll-ups; Q5 DOJ FCA customs; Q6 232 metal-content rule; Q7 legal premise (who may file protest/drawback, contingency); Q8 concentration of duty payers; Q9 bond market; Q10 122/301 litigation status; Q11 Tarifflo; Q12 Altana.

## Stage 1 findings (searches 1-7; 2026-10-18)
- [primary, read PDF] CBP HQ H350722, dated Jan 16, 2026 (internal advice to Detroit CEE): an UNLICENSED online platform doing OCR/extraction of entry data into filings, tying AI classification to 10-digit HTS for a prospective entry, filing CBP Form 5106, or acting as POA intermediary = "customs business" needing a broker licence (19 USC 1641; up to $10,000/transaction penalty per 1641(b)(6) per secondary). 6-digit general classification research + disclaimer (HQ H272798) = OK. => legal moat for LICENSED-broker-native software; unlicensed SaaS (Altana-style, Tarifflo-style) must partner with a licensed broker.
- [secondary, Thompson Hine/BHFS/GHY Apr 2026] Sec 232 restructure effective 2026-04-06: duty now on FULL customs value of metal derivatives, Annex I-A 50%, I-B 25%, Annex III transitional (total 15%) thru 2027-12-31; goods in annexes with <15% aggregate metal weight are NOT subject (does not apply to Ch.72,73,74,76) per CBP CSMS 68253075 (Apr 3 2026); Annex IV thresholds, technical corrections May 6 2026. => a binary weight cliff on full value: proof of metal weight % = huge per-SKU value. Docs/verification rules not found yet.
- [secondary] Drawback: Sec 301 duties drawback-eligible (CSMS 18-000419); Sec 232 steel/aluminum barred except narrow mfg path; IEEPA via CAPE not drawback; 2026 Sec 301 (60 economies) appears eligible after Aug 2026 CBP fix (vendor claim, verify). Paid ~ $0.9-1B/yr historically (GAO avg $896M 2011-18); "unclaimed $15B/yr / 80%" is vendor marketing, unreliable. Drawback vendors: Pax, Consyn, Lightsource, GingerControl, Passport, plus brokers.
- [secondary] Duty financing crowded: Slope (JPM $65M Jul 2024; duty financing apps +730%), Flexport Capital ($100M run rate), HSBC TradePay for Import Duties (May 2025).
- [secondary] AI customs startups: Amari AI ($4.5M seed Feb 2026, 30+ brokerage customers), Alchemize (YC S26, AI-native brokerage, $500K), Nabu (EUR3M May 2026), Digicust (EUR2.3M), iCustoms; Flexport Customs LLC = licensed national-permit broker, standalone brokerage +86% YoY; Flexport customs tech suite Oct 2025. Tarifflo (YC S26; $520K; contingency audits, PSC/protest; broker arrangement unclear; "1 in 8 entries overpaid" self-reported on 1M entries).
- [secondary] DOJ Trade Fraud Task Force (Aug 2025) passed $1B recoveries Jul 14 2026; Perfectus Aluminum $549.5M FCA (May 12 2026, qui tam, relator 17.5%); Ceratizit $54.4M (Dec 2025, Sec 301 origin); trade-fraud qui tam filings surging.
- CBP monthly: Jan 2025 2.9M entry summaries ($338B value), Feb 2.7M, May 2.8M => ~33-35M entry summaries/yr [derivation]. FY2025 CBP audits completed 465 (revised).
- CAPE (IEEPA refund system): 25.1M entries accepted by Jul 31 2026 => free government pipe killed refund-filing intermediaries.

## Stage 2 findings (searches 8-13)
- [secondary] US customs brokerage market ~ $5.48B 2026 (Mordor, paid est.), 2.9% CAGR; CBP FAQ: ~14,454 active licensed brokers; exam pass ~15%; ~6,200 firms (blog, unverified). PE: Platinum Equity bought Livingston (2019); Southfield/Alba Wheels Up bought several brokers (Steer).
- [secondary] Sec 122: CIT struck down May 7 2026 (relief only named plaintiffs), Fed Cir stayed; in force Feb 24-Jul 24 2026; refunds unresolved. Sec 301 forced-labor tariffs (60 economies, 10%/12.5%) effective Jul 24 2026; Burlap & Barrel v US master case + 25-state suit; hearing Sep 30 2026, no ruling found. => protest-preservation window but IEEPA precedent (CBP built free CAPE pipe, 25.1M entries accepted by Jul 31) says intermediaries get squeezed.
- [secondary] No Sec 232 exclusion process since Feb 10 2025 (inclusion windows only); one source says NO exclusion petition path for new 301 (fixed Annex A exemption list). => no 'exclusion request factory'.
- [primary-ish via search of Census rp24/rp25] Related-party imports: 2024 49.5% ($1,610.8B of $3,251.2B imports for consumption); 2025 49.0% ($1,663.1B of $3,391.2B). Includes US firms with foreign subs and US subs of foreign parents.
- [secondary] Customs value of related-party goods usually = intercompany transfer price; CBP accepts via circumstances-of-sale or test values (19 USC 1401a); TP study alone does not satisfy; ACE Reconciliation allows flagging entries and true-up within 21 months, needs pre-existing formula for downward adjustments; IRC 1059A caps tax basis at customs value (whipsaw rule); IRS PLR 202552012 (Dec 26 2025) says 1059A doesn't cap in some deductive-value cases (taxpayer-specific). Penalties 200-800% of loss of revenue (customs firm claim).
- NY Fed (Feb 2026): ~90% of 2025 tariff burden on US firms/consumers; weighted avg statutory rate 2.6% (start 2025) -> 13% (Dec 2025). About 2/3 of importers pay duties in one monthly sum (PMS) per Bloomberg Tax May 2025. Effective collected rate = 214/3391 = ~6.3% of 2025 imports [derivation].
- No source found for duty concentration by importer size (gap; flag as assumption).

## Stage 3 findings (searches 14-15 + fetches)
- [primary, fetched] Census rp25 (Jul 7 2026): 2025 related-party imports $1,663.1B = 49.0% of $3,391.2B imports for consumption; related-party = US firms w/ foreign subs AND US subs of foreign parents.
- [primary, fetched LII] 17 CFR 210.2-01(c)(5): accountant not independent if provides product/service to an audit client for a contingent fee or commission => Big Four cannot do contingency duty recovery for audit clients.
- [primary, fetched LII] 19 USC 1313: drawback = 99%; 5 yrs from importation; (j)(2) claimant must be importer OR have received imported/same-HTS8 merchandise directly/indirectly from importer; mfg substitution claimant = manufacturer who received merchandise directly/indirectly from importer; importer stays liable for claimant up to lesser of duties claimed/authorized (k)(2). => rights travel with the merchandise chain; a FREE-FLOATING drawback rights exchange is NOT legally possible (idea killed); a chain-of-title ledger is.
- [secondary] No software vendor found that reconciles transfer pricing with customs value (only Big Four/law-firm advisory, generic TP software). CBP Reconciliation: flag entries, reconcile within 21 months; needs pre-existing formula for downward adjustments.
- [secondary via fallback search] Altana: CBP 2-yr federal contract (Axios, 2025-10-31); acquired Cervo AI (customs paperwork automation) 2026-07-24; ~ $37.5M est. ARR 2024 (Latka, unreliable), $322M raised; WTO case study: importers opt in to share compliance info with CBP (Product Passports) to pre-validate shipments.
- No drawback rights marketplace found.
- Searches used: 15 WebSearch + 5 WebFetch + 3 fallback-script. Stop searching; design ideas.
